ANSIBLE_DAYS = {
24:{"title":"Introduction to Ansible","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"topic-verified","summary":"Ansible introduced configuration management as a way to describe and repeat system changes over SSH instead of maintaining a collection of one-off login rituals.","architecture":"Control node → inventory → SSH → managed hosts → modules","how":["Ansible is agentless for normal Linux management: the control node connects to managed hosts, gathers the information it needs and executes modules remotely.","The important shift is from typing a sequence of commands manually to describing the state you want and making that description repeatable. A playbook can be reviewed, versioned and rerun instead of living in shell history.","Inventory, authentication and privilege escalation form the path to the target. If any part of that path is wrong, automation has not failed mysteriously; a specific layer has."],"commands":"ansible --version\nansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping","verify":"Run the ping module against the intended inventory and confirm every expected host responds through the exact account and SSH path you intend to automate.","gotcha":"Ansible's ping module is not ICMP ping. It verifies that Ansible can connect and execute its module payload on the managed node.","security":"Use SSH keys, host-key verification and controlled sudo privileges. An automation account that can become unrestricted root everywhere is convenient and correspondingly dangerous.","lesson":"Automation begins with a trustworthy control path before it begins with YAML.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published video evidence; expanded technical record."},
25:{"title":"First Ansible Playbook","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"topic-verified","summary":"The first playbook moved from one-off commands to a declarative file that maps hosts to tasks and desired state.","architecture":"Playbook → play → target hosts → ordered tasks → module results","how":["A play binds a host pattern to a list of tasks. Each task calls a module with parameters that describe the state or action required.","Using modules such as package, file, copy and service is preferable to hiding everything behind shell commands because modules can report whether they actually changed state.","A playbook also becomes documentation: another engineer can see what the machine is supposed to look like instead of reverse-engineering it from the machine itself."],"commands":"ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml\nansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --check","verify":"Run the playbook once, inspect changed/ok results, then run it again. A well-designed state-oriented play should converge and show fewer or no changes on the second run.","gotcha":"Check mode is useful, not omniscient. Some modules cannot fully predict changes and external side effects may not be represented.","security":"Review `become` usage and task scope. A playbook can reproduce a privilege mistake across an entire fleet with impressive consistency.","lesson":"Repeatability is the first visible benefit of infrastructure as code; idempotence is what makes repeatability safe.","evidence":"Day/topic verified by published video evidence; expanded technical record."},
26:{"title":"Ansible Variables","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published","summary":"Variables separate changing data from reusable automation, but precedence decides which value Ansible ultimately uses.","architecture":"defaults / inventory / play vars / task vars / extra vars → precedence → effective value","how":["Variables let a playbook keep logic stable while environment-specific values change. Hostnames, ports, package names and environment flags belong in data when they are not part of the reusable task logic.","Ansible has multiple variable sources, so precedence matters. A value can appear correct in one file and still be overridden by a higher-precedence source at runtime.","Extra variables passed with `-e` have very high precedence and are useful for deliberate overrides, but that same power can hide the value the repository appears to declare."],"commands":"ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml -e 'app_port=8080'\nansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --host web01","verify":"Print or debug the effective variable in a lab, then inspect the inventory/host data that supplied it. Do not debug precedence by staring at one YAML file.","gotcha":"A variable defined in the 'obvious' place can still lose to another source. Always reason about the effective value, not the file you last edited.","security":"Do not store secrets in plain variable files. Use Vault or an external secret mechanism and keep secret precedence deliberate.","lesson":"Variables make automation reusable; precedence makes it necessary to know where truth actually comes from.","evidence":"Published artifact and conversation evidence."},
27:{"title":"Ansible Inventory","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published","summary":"Inventory is the model of what Ansible manages: hosts, groups and the connection data that lets automation reach the correct systems.","architecture":"inventory → groups / hosts → host variables → play host pattern","how":["An inventory can be static or dynamic, but its job is the same: identify managed hosts and organize them into useful groups.","Groups should model real operational boundaries such as web, database, environment or region rather than becoming arbitrary buckets. Group membership then becomes reusable targeting logic.","Connection variables belong only where necessary. Keeping target identity and connection assumptions explicit prevents a playbook from silently reaching the wrong machine."],"commands":"ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --graph\nansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --list","verify":"Render the inventory graph and confirm every host belongs to the groups you expect before running a broad play.","gotcha":"A typo in a group name can produce a perfectly valid pattern that matches zero hosts. 'Nothing changed' may mean 'nothing was targeted.'","security":"Keep private keys and credentials outside inventory files. Inventory often ends up in source control and should be safe to publish internally.","lesson":"Targeting starts with an accurate model of the fleet.","evidence":"Published conversation evidence."},
28:{"title":"Ansible Patterns","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published","summary":"Patterns decide which inventory hosts a command or play targets, making selection logic part of the safety model.","architecture":"inventory set + host pattern → matched host set → task execution","how":["Patterns can target groups, individual hosts, intersections, exclusions and combinations. This makes them powerful enough to describe production subsets without duplicating playbooks.","A safe pattern is one you can explain before you run it. For broad changes, list the matches first rather than discovering the set while the change is already in progress.","Patterns are especially valuable when environment groups are modeled cleanly in inventory because targeting then expresses intent instead of host-by-host maintenance."],"commands":"ansible 'web:&prod' -i inventory.ini --list-hosts\nansible 'all:!db' -i inventory.ini --list-hosts","verify":"Use `--list-hosts` before the real command or play and compare the result against the exact systems you intended to change.","gotcha":"Exclusion and intersection syntax is compact enough to be misread. Preview the matched set instead of trusting punctuation under pressure.","security":"Treat host selection as a change-control boundary. The same task against the wrong group can become a security incident.","lesson":"Automation safety is partly about writing the task and partly about proving who will receive it.","evidence":"Published conversation evidence."},
29:{"title":"Ansible Ad-Hoc Commands","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published","summary":"Ad-hoc commands provide fast remote execution for small tasks without turning every inspection or one-time action into a playbook.","architecture":"ansible CLI → host pattern → one module invocation → result per host","how":["Ad-hoc commands are useful for reachability checks, package inspection, service state and small fleet-wide operations.","They still use modules, inventory, variables and privilege escalation, so the same targeting and security rules apply as with playbooks.","When a command becomes repeatable, operationally important or hard to explain from shell history, it has outgrown ad-hoc use and belongs in version-controlled automation."],"commands":"ansible web -i inventory.ini -m command -a 'uptime'\nansible all -i inventory.ini -m package_facts\nansible web -i inventory.ini -m service_facts","verify":"Inspect per-host return data and failed/unreachable counts. Do not reduce a fleet-wide command to whether your terminal prompt returned.","gotcha":"The `command` module does not invoke a shell, so shell operators such as pipes and redirection will not behave as if you typed them into Bash.","security":"Do not pass sensitive values on command lines that may be logged in shell history or process listings.","lesson":"Fast does not mean disposable: targeting and evidence still matter for one-off automation.","evidence":"Published conversation evidence."},
30:{"title":"Ansible Modules","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published","summary":"Modules are Ansible's state-aware building blocks and are generally safer than converting every operation into shell commands.","architecture":"task → module + arguments → remote state inspection/change → structured result","how":["Modules encode domain behavior for files, packages, services, users, clouds and many other resources. They can report `changed`, `failed` and structured result fields instead of leaving you to infer state from text output.","State-oriented modules support idempotence: installing an already installed package or enforcing an existing file mode can return `ok` instead of pretending work happened again.","The shell and command modules remain useful when no suitable module exists, but they should be chosen intentionally because you then own idempotence, parsing and failure semantics."],"commands":"ansible-doc package\nansible-doc service\nansible all -i inventory.ini -m package -a 'name=curl state=present' -b","verify":"Run the module twice in a lab and inspect whether the second run correctly reports no change when the desired state already exists.","gotcha":"A shell command returning exit code 0 only proves the command considered itself successful. It does not necessarily prove the desired system state exists.","security":"Prefer modules that avoid shell interpolation when possible; it reduces accidental command injection and quoting mistakes.","lesson":"Use the highest-level reliable primitive that expresses the state you want.","evidence":"Published conversation and carousel evidence."},
31:{"title":"Ansible Facts","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published","summary":"Facts let automation discover the host it is operating on instead of hard-coding assumptions about every machine.","architecture":"setup / fact gathering → ansible_facts → condition / template / task decision","how":["Fact gathering exposes operating-system, network, memory, architecture and other host information as structured variables.","Facts allow one playbook to branch appropriately across distributions or use discovered interface and filesystem information without separate copies of the automation.","Gather only what you need when scale or performance matters. Facts are useful because they make automation adapt to reality, not because more data is automatically better."],"commands":"ansible all -i inventory.ini -m setup\nansible all -i inventory.ini -m setup -a 'filter=ansible_distribution*'","verify":"Compare selected facts with host commands such as `/etc/os-release`, `ip addr` or `uname` in a lab to understand what the fact represents.","gotcha":"Facts describe the host Ansible reached, which is another reason correct inventory targeting matters. The wrong server can provide perfectly valid facts.","security":"Facts may expose interface, hostname and system metadata; handle logs and artifacts accordingly in sensitive environments.","lesson":"Good automation observes before it assumes.","evidence":"Published conversation evidence."},
32:{"title":"Ansible Roles","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published","summary":"Roles break large playbooks into reusable units with conventional locations for tasks, handlers, defaults, templates and files.","architecture":"site playbook → roles → tasks / handlers / templates / defaults / files","how":["A role gives related automation a predictable structure. Instead of one playbook accumulating every responsibility, web-server logic, database logic and common host setup can evolve independently.","Role defaults provide low-precedence configurable values, while role tasks describe the behavior. This separation makes a role reusable without making its internals unreadable.","The published Day 32 teaser promised Galaxy next, but the journey later paid that teaser debt at Day 39. The blog preserves that history rather than pretending the sequence never bent."],"commands":"ansible-galaxy role init roles/web\nansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml","verify":"Run the role through a small top-level playbook, inspect handler behavior and confirm a second run converges cleanly.","gotcha":"A role can become a dumping ground if responsibilities are not bounded. Reusability comes from a coherent purpose, not simply moving files into `roles/`.","security":"Keep role defaults non-secret and make privileged tasks obvious. Reusable automation also reuses mistakes at scale.","lesson":"Structure is operational leverage when it makes behavior easier to reason about and test.","evidence":"Published video/carousel evidence. Historical teaser debt recorded forward and paid at Day 39."},
33:{"title":"host_vars, group_vars & register","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published","summary":"Real projects need static per-host/group data and runtime results without mixing both into one giant variable file.","architecture":"group_vars + host_vars + task output(register) → effective runtime data → later tasks","how":["`group_vars` stores data shared by inventory groups and `host_vars` stores data specific to individual hosts. The directory structure keeps inventory-related data close to the model it describes.","`register` captures the structured result of a task for later decisions. Return data can include stdout, stderr, return codes, changed state and module-specific fields.","Static inventory data and runtime observations solve different problems. Keeping them separate makes it easier to see which value is configuration and which value was discovered during this execution."],"commands":"ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --host web01\nansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml -vv","verify":"Use a debug task in a lab to inspect a registered result, then remove broad debug output before production if it could expose sensitive data.","gotcha":"Registered variables exist for the current playbook run; they are not a durable state store. If you need persistent truth, put it somewhere designed to persist.","security":"Registered output can contain tokens, command output or other secrets. Use `no_log` appropriately and avoid dumping whole result objects into CI logs.","lesson":"Automation gets clearer when configuration data and observed runtime data have different homes.","evidence":"Published video/carousel evidence."},
34:{"title":"Conditionals in Ansible","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published","summary":"Conditionals make tasks depend on facts and results, while `failed_when` lets automation define what failure actually means for a command.","architecture":"fact / registered result → when / failed_when → run, skip or fail task","how":["`when` controls whether a task executes based on variables, facts or earlier results. This lets one play adapt to the host rather than force every machine through identical operations.","`failed_when` is crucial when a command's return code does not map cleanly to success/failure. A tool can use a non-zero code to mean 'changes found' or another expected state.","The opposite trap also exists: `ignore_errors` can keep a play moving while hiding a failure that should have stopped the operation. Failure semantics should be encoded, not waved away."],"commands":"# Example logic\n# failed_when: result.rc not in [0, 2]\n# changed_when: result.rc == 2","verify":"Exercise both branches deliberately: one result that should pass and one that should fail. A conditional you only test on the happy path is still a hypothesis.","gotcha":"`ignore_errors` is not recovery logic. It can turn a broken step into a later, harder-to-diagnose failure.","security":"Conditionals often guard privileged or environment-specific tasks. Verify the source variable cannot be trivially manipulated by an untrusted input.","lesson":"Reliable automation defines failure in terms of the tool's real behavior rather than assuming every non-zero code means the same thing.","evidence":"Published video evidence; the published teaser committed Day 35 to Templates."},
35:{"title":"Jinja2 Templates","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published-after-ledger","summary":"Jinja2 templates generate configuration from variables so the structure is reusable while environment-specific values remain data.","architecture":"template.j2 + variables → Ansible template module → rendered config → service","how":["The `template` module renders Jinja2 expressions on the control side and writes the resulting file to the managed host with ownership and mode controls.","Templates are strongest when they express configuration, not when they become hidden programs. Keep branching limited and push complicated data shaping into variables or dedicated logic where it is easier to test.","A rendered configuration often needs validation before service reload. That is where the template module's validation support or an explicit check becomes part of safe deployment."],"commands":"ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml\n# task uses ansible.builtin.template: src=nginx.conf.j2 dest=/etc/nginx/nginx.conf","verify":"Render into a test path or use the application's native config validator before allowing a handler to reload the service.","gotcha":"A syntactically valid template can render syntactically invalid application configuration. Jinja success is not Nginx/Apache/application validation.","security":"Avoid rendering secrets into world-readable files and set destination ownership/mode explicitly.","lesson":"Templating separates reusable structure from changing values, but the rendered artifact is what the service actually consumes.","evidence":"Later journey continuity shows this day was published after the older ledger snapshot; expanded record preserves the Day 34 teaser sequence."},
36:{"title":"Ansible Handlers","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published-after-ledger","summary":"Handlers trigger follow-up actions such as service reloads only when notifying tasks actually change something.","architecture":"task changes → notify → handler queued → handler runs at handler flush/end of play","how":["A handler is typically used for actions that should only happen after a meaningful configuration change, such as reloading Nginx after its configuration file changed.","Multiple tasks can notify the same handler and Ansible de-duplicates the notification so the service does not restart after every tiny task.","Handlers normally run later in the play. That timing matters if later tasks require the new configuration immediately; use the design intentionally instead of assuming notification means instant execution."],"commands":"# task: notify: Reload nginx\n# handler: ansible.builtin.service: name=nginx state=reloaded","verify":"Run once with a changed template and confirm the handler runs; run again without changes and confirm the handler is not triggered.","gotcha":"If a play fails before notified handlers run, the expected reload may never happen unless handler behavior is deliberately designed, for example with force-handlers where appropriate.","security":"Validate configuration before notifying a privileged service reload. Automation should not efficiently reload a broken config.","lesson":"Change-driven actions are safer than unconditional restarts.","evidence":"Published later in the journey; older draft was misnumbered and was corrected forward."},
37:{"title":"Ansible Loops","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published-after-ledger","summary":"Loops reduce repetitive tasks while keeping each item visible in Ansible's result model.","architecture":"task + iterable items → one module invocation per item → per-item results","how":["Loops are useful for repetitive resources such as users, directories or small sets of configuration entries.","They should not replace a module's native ability to accept a list when batching is clearer or more efficient. Package modules, for example, can often receive a list of packages in one task.","Registered loop results are nested per item, so later logic must understand the result structure instead of assuming it looks like a single task result."],"commands":"# loop:\n#   - alice\n#   - bob\n# ansible.builtin.user:\n#   name: '{{ item }}'\n#   state: present","verify":"Inspect `changed` and failed state per item and rerun to confirm the loop converges rather than recreating work.","gotcha":"Looping a package-install task one package at a time can be an unnecessary anti-pattern when the module accepts the complete package list directly.","security":"When items come from external data, validate them before using them in paths, shell commands or privileged resource names.","lesson":"A loop should make intent shorter without making result handling harder to understand.","evidence":"Published later in the journey; the old misnumbered draft was corrected before publication."},
38:{"title":"Blocks, Rescue & Always","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Blocks group related tasks, `rescue` provides controlled recovery for task failures inside the block, and `always` guarantees cleanup or logging paths.","architecture":"block → success path\n     ↘ task failure → rescue\nblock/rescue completion → always","how":["A `block` groups the tasks that form one logical operation. If a task inside that block fails, `rescue` can restore a backup, record failure context or perform another deliberate recovery path.","`always` runs regardless of whether the normal block succeeded or the rescue path executed, making it useful for cleanup and final logging.","The published example used a deployment block, a rescue path that restored a previous backup, and an explicit `fail` after rollback so the pipeline would not report the failed deployment as success."],"commands":"- name: Deploy application\n  block:\n    - name: Copy files\n      ansible.builtin.copy:\n        src: app/\n        dest: /opt/app/\n  rescue:\n    - name: Restore backup\n      ansible.builtin.command: /opt/scripts/restore_backup.sh\n    - name: Rolled back deploy is still a failure\n      ansible.builtin.fail:\n        msg: 'Deployment failed and was rolled back'\n  always:\n    - name: Record completion\n      ansible.builtin.debug:\n        msg: 'Deployment path finished'","verify":"Force a task inside the block to fail in a lab. Confirm rescue executes, always executes, and the final play result reflects failure when rollback means the deployment did not succeed.","gotcha":"`rescue` does not catch everything: unreachable hosts, parse errors and failures outside the block are not rescued. Also, a successful rescue can make the play continue green unless you deliberately re-fail the operation.","security":"Recovery scripts are privileged code paths too. Protect backups, validate restore sources and avoid logging secrets in failure context.","lesson":"Design the recovery path before production tests your optimism.","evidence":"Published Day 38 infographic artifact recovered from the project archive."},
39:{"title":"Ansible Galaxy Collections","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Collections package modules, plugins, roles, playbooks and documentation so teams can reuse maintained automation instead of rebuilding every integration from scratch.","architecture":"requirements / Galaxy → collection install → fully-qualified module name → playbook","how":["Collections are distributed bundles maintained by Ansible, vendors and the community. They extend the modules and plugins available to playbooks without copying implementation code into every repository.","The published example installed `amazon.aws` and then used a fully qualified module such as `amazon.aws.ec2_instance`.","Production use should make dependency versions deliberate. A collection is external code in your automation supply chain; pin and review it rather than assuming 'latest' is a stability strategy."],"commands":"ansible-galaxy collection install amazon.aws\nansible-galaxy collection list\n# Example module: amazon.aws.ec2_instance","verify":"List installed collections, run `ansible-doc` for the exact fully-qualified module and test the pinned collection version in a non-production target before fleet rollout.","gotcha":"A collection name that works today can change behavior after an unpinned upgrade. Reuse saves work; dependency drift can quietly reintroduce it.","security":"Review publisher/source, pin trusted versions, and treat collection updates like code dependency updates.","lesson":"Do not reinvent mature integrations, but do not outsource dependency control either.","evidence":"Published Day 39 infographic artifact. This day also paid the Galaxy teaser debt printed on Day 32."},
40:{"title":"LAMP Automation Capstone","phase":"Infrastructure Automation","status":"published","summary":"The Ansible phase closed by combining roles for common setup, Apache, PHP and MySQL into a deployable LAMP stack with Vault, handlers and failure recovery.","architecture":"control node → site.yml → common/apache/php/mysql roles → target host → HTTP + database services","how":["The capstone moved beyond isolated syntax and connected reusable roles into one deployment. Common host setup, web server configuration, PHP and database responsibilities were separated so each role remained understandable.","Sensitive values were kept out of plain playbook logic with Vault. Handlers connected configuration changes to service actions instead of restarting services unconditionally.","The lab also exposed real operational failures: package-manager return behavior around MySQL and a port-80 conflict later in the deployment. Those failures are more valuable evidence than a perfect demo because they force the automation to encode what recovery and verification actually mean."],"commands":"ansible-playbook -i hosts.ini site.yml --ask-vault-pass\nansible all -i hosts.ini -m ping","verify":"Verify Ansible reachability first, run the play, then test the HTTP endpoint, expected services and database state from the target. Re-run to confirm convergence after the environment is corrected.","gotcha":"The capstone hit package/return-code and port-conflict realities. Automation must distinguish an expected package-manager condition from an actual failed desired state, and a service cannot bind a port another process already owns.","security":"Keep database secrets encrypted, minimize sudo scope, firewall the host and expose only the services the architecture requires.","lesson":"A capstone earns its value when multiple layers fail together and the operator can still explain exactly which layer broke.","evidence":"Published phase capstone evidence and project failure history."}
}
